  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Land Reform Support Programme</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CPT Paraiba is the Pastoral Land Commission of the Brazilian Bishops conference. Paraiba is located in the North Eastern tip of Brazil, an area of extreme poverty and major land conflict. CPT provides pastoral, technical, legal, political and social assistance to poor rural communities with a particular emphasis on improving the lives of women and young people. CPT Paraiba is organised into 4 geographical teams in Agreste, Vale, Varzea and Litoral. The CPT work in 59 communities, of which 52 are assentamentos ( permanent settlements) and 7 are acampamentos (encampments/temporary settlements), i.e. areas of occupation where families need support to help get legal recognition of the land and defend themselves from eviction. These are: Antas, Arintingui, Mangue, Marinas, Ponta de Gramame, Salgadinho, and Tambauzinho.
This project, which is implemented by CPT Joao Pessoa (part of the wider network of CPT Paraiba), aims to support 623 small farmers, including 271 women that are part of 4 women&#x2019;s groups,  who live in the state of Paraiba to increase their food security, remain on their land and preserve the environment. CPT will support rural families to become self-sufficient by training them in the use of organic environmentally friendly agricultural techniques to increase crop quality and production, support their participation and leadership in farmers&#x2019; cooperatives and associations, and increase the commercialisation of their produce at local eco-fairs and markets. The project also focuses on community organisation to access three key governmental agricultural commercialisation programmes: PAA (the Acquisition of Food Programme), PNAE (The National Programme for School Food), and CDL (Direct Local Purchase).</narrative>
  </description>
